  "issue": {
    "body": "### Bug\n\n`FileResponse._parse_range_header` rejects a Range only when `start > end`, but `bytes=5-4` parses to the empty half-open range `(5, 5)` and slips past that guard. The result is a malformed `206 Partial Content` (`Content-Length: 0`, a backwards `Content-Range: bytes 5-4/...`) instead of a `400`.\n\n`bytes=10-5` is caught (it parses to `(10, 6)`, so `10 > 6`), but the off-by-one boundary case where `end + 1 == start` is not.\n\n### Reproduce\n\n```python\nfrom starlette.responses import FileResponse\nFileResponse._parse_range_header(\"bytes=5-4\", 100)   # -> [(5, 5)]  (accepted; should be 400)\nFileResponse._parse_range_header(\"bytes=5-5\", 100)   # -> [(5, 6)]  (valid one-byte range)\nFileResponse._parse_range_header(\"bytes=10-5\", 100)  # -> MalformedRangeHeader (already correct)\n```\n\n### Fix\n\nThe guard should be `start >= end` (an empty range is not satisfiable). A genuine one-byte request `bytes=5-5` parses to `(5, 6)` and is unaffected.\n\nI have a fix + tests ready and opened it as a draft PR for reference. Happy to adjust or drop it if you'd prefer to handle it differently.\n",
    "body_last_edited_at": null,
